WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:05.470
Machine learning is creating tremendous economic value today.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:10.990
I think 99% of the economic value created by machine learning today is through one type

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:13.950
of machine learning, which is called supervised learning.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:17.690
Let's take a look at what that means.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:22.650
Supervised machine learning, or more commonly supervised learning, refers to algorithms

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:28.890
that learn x to y, or input to output mappings.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:35.630
The key characteristic of supervised learning is that you give your learning algorithm examples

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:42.610
to learn from that includes the right answers, whereby right answer, I mean the correct label

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:45.610
y for a given input x.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:53.210
And it's by seeing correct pairs of input x and desired output label y that the learning

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:58.850
algorithm eventually learns to take just the input alone without the output label and gives

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:03.850
a reasonably accurate prediction or guess of the output.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.530
Let's look at some examples.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:14.530
If the input x is an email and the output y is this email spam or not spam, this gives

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:17.730
you your spam filter.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:26.570
Or if the input is an audio clip and the algorithm's job is output the text transcript,

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:30.370
then this is speech recognition.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:36.530
Or if you want to input English and have it output the corresponding Spanish, Arabic, Hindi,

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:43.370
Chinese, Japanese, or something else translation, then that's machine translation.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:48.810
For the most lucrative form of supervised learning today is probably used in online

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.890
advertising.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:56.490
Nearly all the large online ad platforms have a learning algorithm that inputs some information

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:02.290
about an ad and some information about you, and then tries to figure out if you will

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:04.730
click on that ad or not.

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:08.690
Because by showing you ads that you're slightly more likely to click on for these large

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:12.210
online ad platforms every click is revenue.

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:15.650
This actually drives a lot of revenue for these companies.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:20.810
This is something I once done a lot of work on, maybe not the most inspiring application,

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:25.690
but it certainly has a significant economic impact in some companies today.

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:31.090
Or if you want to build a self-driving car, the learning algorithm would take as input

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:37.090
an image and some information from other sensors such as a radar or other things,

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:42.650
and then try to output the position of say other cars so that your self-driving car can

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:45.810
safely drive around the other cars.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:46.810
Or take manufacturing.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:52.410
I've actually done a lot of work in this sector at Landing AI.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:58.050
You can have a learning algorithm takes as input a picture of a manufactured product, say

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:03.730
a cell phone that just rolled off the production line and have the learning algorithm output

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:09.090
whether or not there is a scratch, dent or other defects in the product.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:14.010
This is called visual inspection and is helping manufacturers reduce or prevent defects in

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:16.110
their products.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:21.730
In all of these applications, you would first train your model with examples of inputs X

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:26.050
and the right answers, that is the labels Y.

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:31.570
After the model has learned from these input outputs or X and Y pairs, it can then take

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:36.810
a brand new input X, something it's never seen before, and try to produce the appropriate

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:38.570
corresponding output Y.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:45.210
Let's dive more deeply into one specific example.

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:50.290
Say you want to predict housing prices based on the size of the house.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:55.970
You've collected some data and say you plot the data and it looks like this.

00:03:55.970 --> 00:04:00.130
Here on the horizontal axis is the size of the house in square feet.

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:03.170
And yes, I live in the United States where we still use square feet.

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:07.010
I know most of the world uses square meters.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:13.410
And here on the vertical axis is the price of the house in say thousands of dollars.

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:20.050
So with this data, let's say a friend wants to know what's the price for their 750 square

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:21.550
foot house.

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:24.170
How can a learning algorithm help you?

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:29.810
One thing a learning algorithm might be able to do is say fit a straight line to the data

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:34.330
and reading off the straight line, it looks like your friend's house could be sold for

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:39.290
maybe about, I don't know, $150,000.

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:43.150
But fitting a straight line isn't the only learning algorithm you can use.

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:46.750
There are others that could work better for this application.

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:51.050
For example, rather than fitting a straight line, you might decide that it's better

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:57.130
to fit a curve, a function that's slightly more complicated or more complex than a straight

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:58.650
line.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:03.230
If you do that and make a prediction here, then it looks like, well, your friend's

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:08.470
house could be sold for closer to $200,000.

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:13.730
One of the things you see later in this class is how you can decide whether to fit a straight

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:20.930
line, a curve or another function that is even more complex to the data.

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:25.790
Now it doesn't seem appropriate to pick the one that gives your friend the best price.

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:31.930
The one thing you see is how to get an algorithm to systematically choose the most appropriate

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:37.250
line or curve or other thing to fit to this data.

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:41.910
What you see in this slide is an example of supervised learning.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:46.990
Because we gave the algorithm a data set in which the so called right answer, that is

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:52.650
the label or the correct price Y is given for every house on the plot.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:58.570
And the task of the learning algorithm is to produce more of these right answers, specifically

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:04.050
predicting what is the likely price for other houses like your friend's house.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.850
That's why this is supervised learning.

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:11.730
To define a little bit more terminology, this housing price prediction is a particular type

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:14.930
of supervised learning called regression.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:20.490
And by regression, I mean we're trying to predict a number from infinitely many possible

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:31.130
numbers, such as the house prices in our example, which could be 150,000 or 70,000 or 183,000

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:34.090
or any other number in between.

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:39.890
So that's supervised learning, learning input output or x to y mappings.

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:46.250
And you saw in this video an example of regression with the task is to predict a number.

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:52.530
But there's also a second major type of supervised learning problem called classification.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.850
Let's take a look at what that means in the next video.
